    3 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Manual Handling and Back Care',
                'summary' => 'Lift so your back survives the season.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Coconut work moves heavy things: seed nuts, fertiliser bags, harvested bunches, and water containers. Back injuries are the most common field injury, and they are almost always the result of how the lift was done, not the weight itself.\n\nStart with the lift itself. Stand close to the load, feet apart, and bend the knees, not the back. Grip the load firmly, keep it close to the body, and lift with the legs. The back is a crane with a poor hinge; the legs are the engine. A load lifted with the back is a load the back pays for later.\n\nSize the load before you lift it. A bag of fertiliser that is too heavy for one person is a two-person job, and asking is not weakness; it is the correct technique. Split the load, use a wheelbarrow, or make two trips. The back that is injured today is the farm that is short a worker for weeks.\n\nCarry and set down with the same care. Turn with the feet, not the twist of the spine, and set the load down by reversing the lift. Repetition is the silent multiplier: ten correct lifts an hour become a thousand a week, and the technique is what makes the thousand safe.\n\nFinally, build the habit of the warm-up and the pause. Stretch before heavy work, and rest between heavy lifts. The worker who lifts well every time is the worker who finishes the season standing straight, and a straight back is the farm's most valuable equipment.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Working at Heights: Poles and Ladders',
                'summary' => 'Climb and cut without the fall.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Harvest work happens above the ground, and falls are the most serious hazard on a coconut farm. The worker who treats height as a system, not an improvisation, is the worker who comes down whole.\n\nStart with the equipment. Inspect the pole or ladder before use: no cracks, no loose rungs, no worn feet, and a stable base. A ladder on wet or soft ground is a ladder that slips; level the footing or choose a different approach. The two-minute inspection prevents the fall that ends the harvest.\n\nSet up the work before climbing. Clear the ground below, keep bystanders away, and plan where you will stand and where the bunch will fall. The climber who plans the descent is the climber who is not surprised by it, and most falls happen during the awkward final reach, not the climb.\n\nClimb with three points of contact, and carry nothing in your hands while climbing. Use a belt or a rope for tools, and never climb with a knife in your hand. Cut so the bunch falls clear of you, and never stand directly under a cut bunch or a loose frond.\n\nFinally, respect the conditions. Stop climbing in rain, high wind, or when tired; the tired climb is the dangerous climb. The worker who checks the gear, plans the fall zone, and climbs with three points of contact is the worker who finishes the season with both feet on the ground.",
            ],
        ],
