    5 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Grading and Quality Basics',
                'summary' => 'Grade so buyers know what they are buying.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Grade is the language of the marketplace, and the seller who grades consistently sells with confidence. Buyers filter by grade, pay by grade, and return produce that does not match its grade, so grading is a skill worth learning properly.\n\nStart with the standard grades for your product. For coconut produce, grade by size, maturity, and condition: medium, large, and extra-large; fresh, husked, or de-husked; sound, cracked, or damaged. Learn the grade your farm produces most of, and describe it with the words buyers use, because the listing that speaks the buyer's language is the listing that converts.\n\nGrade at harvest, not at sale. Sort at the collection point while the produce is fresh, and keep the grades separate. Mixed grades sell at the lowest price, because the buyer prices the worst thing in the sack. The seller who sorts at harvest captures the premium the good nuts earned.\n\nInspect before you list. A grade claim is a promise, and the promise is checked at delivery. Photograph a sample of the grade, and describe honestly what the buyer will receive, including the seasonal variation that every farm has. Honest grading builds repeat buyers; optimistic grading builds disputes.\n\nFinally, keep a simple grade card: the grade, the size range, the condition, and the price. The seller who grades by card grades consistently, and consistency is what turns a first buyer into a regular one.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Packing Produce for Delivery',
                'summary' => 'Pack so the produce arrives as it left.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Packing is where good produce becomes a bad delivery. Nuts that left the farm sound but arrived cracked are a refund, a bad review, and a lost customer, and the difference is almost always the packing.\n\nStart with the container. Use clean, dry sacks, crates, or bags that suit the product: breathable for fresh produce, sturdy for bulk, and closed against rain and pests. A container that has held chemicals, or that is damp, contaminates the produce it is meant to protect. The container is the first layer of quality.\n\nNext, protect against movement. Fill the container properly, cushion the top layer, and avoid overfilling, which crushes the lower nuts. For de-husked or fresh nuts, keep them off the ground and out of direct sun during packing, and never pack wet produce; moisture is the enemy that turns a good delivery into mould.\n\nLabel and document. Mark the grade, the count or weight, and the order reference on the container, and photograph the packed consignment before handover. The photo is the evidence that settles any dispute about what was dispatched, and the label is what keeps the order and the delivery matched.\n\nFinally, hand over cleanly. Confirm the quantity and grade with the driver or the buyer at handover, and keep the reference. The seller who packs to a standard delivers a product that arrives as it left, and that is the seller whose buyers return.",
            ],
