            [
                'title' => 'Chemical Safety and Spill Response',
                'summary' => 'Handle, mix, and clean up chemicals safely.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Chemicals are a tool with real risk, and the worker who handles them safely gets the benefit without the harm. The rules are simple, and they apply to mixing, spraying, and cleanup alike.\n\nStart with the label. The label is the law and the instruction: the rate, the PPE, the wind conditions, and the pre-harvest interval. Read it before opening the container, every time, because the product that was safe last season may have different instructions this season. Never estimate a rate by eye or by habit.\n\nMix outdoors or in a ventilated area, wearing the PPE the label requires: gloves, boots, long sleeves, and eye protection. Measure with the labelled measure, never by taste or smell, and never eat, drink, or smoke while mixing. The mixer who follows the label is the mixer who goes home healthy.\n\nKnow the spill response: stop the flow if you can do it safely, contain the spill with soil or absorbent, keep it away from water sources and food, and wash the skin with clean water if contacted. Report the spill to your supervisor, and dispose of the residue and the container as the label directs. A small spill handled properly is a non-event; a small spill ignored is a poisoned well.\n\nFinally, store chemicals locked, labelled, and away from food, feed, and water, and triple-rinse empty containers before disposal. The crew that handles chemicals by the label is the crew that the cooperative trusts with the work.",
