    137 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Seedling Grading and Sales',
                'summary' => 'Grade seedlings so buyers pay for quality.',
                'duration' => 40,
                'content' => "Seedling sales reward the nursery that grades honestly. Buyers pay for uniform, healthy, well-documented seedlings, and the nursery that grades by a clear standard sells at a better price with far fewer disputes.\n\nStart with the grading criteria: height, stem girth, leaf count, root health, and freedom from disease. A grade-one seedling is tall for its age, thick-stemmed, with full green fronds and a healthy root mass. Grade at transplanting readiness, not before, because a seedling graded too young is a seedling whose grade changes.\n\nGrade into clear classes: grade one, grade two, and culls. Grade two is a healthy but smaller or slightly uneven seedling that sells at a lower price for interplanting; culls are diseased or deformed and must not be sold as planting stock. The nursery that sells culls as grade one sells a bad reputation with them.\n\nDocument every batch: source palm, sowing date, germination count, and grade counts. The buyer of a certified nursery asks for the batch record, and the record is what turns a good seedling into a premium sale. Buyers pay for provenance because provenance is the only guarantee of what the seedling will become.\n\nFinally, price by grade, not by hope. Check what the market pays for grade one in your region, and price grade two below it. The nursery that grades honestly and prices by grade builds a buyer list that returns every planting season, and repeat buyers are the nursery's real asset.",
