                'content' => "A nursery is a business, and the operator who keeps nursery records runs a business; the one who does not runs a garden with costs. The records are simple, and they are the difference between profit and surprise.\n\nStart with the batch record: source palm, nut grade, sowing date, media used, germination count, and first and full germination dates. The batch record is the nursery's product history, and it is what a serious buyer asks for before ordering hundreds of seedlings.\n\nNext, track the costs: nuts, media, trays, water, shade, labour, and transport. Cost per seedling is the number that decides whether the nursery makes money, and it is the number most operators never calculate. A nursery that knows its cost per seedling prices to profit, not to guess.\n\nTrack sales by batch and grade, and keep the buyer list with contact, quantity, and season. The buyer list is the nursery's future: a buyer who bought grade one last season is the buyer to call before the next season's seedlings are even germinated.\n\nFinally, read the records each season. Which batches germinated best, which source palms produced the best seedlings, and which grade sold fastest. The nursery that reads its records improves every season, and the improving nursery is the one the cooperative certifies first.",
