    138 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Weed Management and Mulching',
                'summary' => 'Keep the palm basin clean without chemicals.',
                'duration' => 40,
                'content' => "Weeds steal water and nutrients from young palms, harbour pests, and complicate harvest, and the grower who manages them without relying on chemicals saves money and protects the soil. Mulching and manual management are the foundation.\n\nStart with the palm basin. Keep a clear, weed-free circle around each young palm, because weeds within a metre of the trunk compete directly for water and nutrients. Slash or hand-weed the basin regularly; herbicides near young palms are risky and can kill the very palms they are meant to protect.\n\nMulch to suppress the rest. Spread husks, fronds, or compost around the palm, keeping the mulch a short distance from the trunk so the base does not rot. A thick mulch suppresses weeds, holds moisture through the dry spells, and feeds soil life as it breaks down. The mulched palm needs less weeding and less watering, which is the whole point.\n\nManage the intercrop as a weed ally. Short-cycle intercrops shade the ground and suppress weeds, but keep them outside the palm basin so they do not compete with the palm. Turn intercrop residues into mulch rather than burning them; burning wastes the organic matter the soil needs.\n\nFinally, watch for the weeds that signal problems: a sudden flush of a tough perennial, or weeds that thrive in waterlogged ground. The weed is a message about the soil, and the grower who reads the message adjusts the drainage or the nutrition. The managed farm is the farm where weeds are read, not just slashed.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Preparing for GAP Verification',
                'summary' => 'Turn Good Agricultural Practice into proof.',
                'duration' => 40,
                'content' => "GAP verification is where your farming practice meets the buyer's requirement, and the farm that arrives prepared passes in one visit while the farm that arrives empty-handed makes the verifier come back. Preparation is a season-long habit with a one-day payoff.\n\nStart with the records the verifier will ask for: the input log, the pest and treatment log, the harvest log, and the farm profile. Every entry should show the date, the product or activity, the block, and the person who did it. The verifier samples the records against the fields, so the record and the farm must agree.\n\nNext, walk the farm as the verifier will. Check that the blocks match the profile, that the storage is clean and separated, that chemical containers are labelled and locked, and that the harvest handling is what the log claims. The farm that matches its records is the farm that passes the spot-check.\n\nPrepare the people as well. Workers should know the safety rules, the PPE requirements, and where the records are kept. A verifier who asks a worker about the last treatment and gets a confident answer is a verifier who believes the whole record.\n\nFinally, fix the gaps before the visit, not during it. A missing log entry reconstructed on the spot is a gap the verifier notes; a complete log is a pass. The farm that treats GAP as a daily practice turns verification into a formality, and the verified farm is the farm that wins the premium market.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    147 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Using Records to Plan Next Season',
                'summary' => 'Turn this season\'s data into next season\'s plan.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "The season's records are next season's plan, and the grower who reads them before planning is the grower who plans with evidence instead of habit. Planning from records is the single highest-return habit in farming.\n\nStart with the yield log by block. Which blocks produced the most, which produced the least, and how does each block compare to its own last season? A block that fell needs a cause: nutrition, water, pests, or age. The record points at the block; the walk through it points at the cause, and the cause points at the fix.\n\nNext, read the input log against the yield. Which fertiliser, which timing, which pest treatment actually moved the yield? The comparison turns spending into investment data: the input that returned its cost gets funded again, and the input that did not gets replaced. The grower who reads this pair of logs stops buying on habit.\n\nThen plan the labour and the cash. The records show which months were heavy with work and which months brought the money. Plan the hiring around the heavy months and the purchases around the cash, and keep the reserve the records say you need for the lean gap.\n\nFinally, write next season's plan from the records: the blocks to rehabilitate, the inputs to change, the varieties to favour, and the one improvement that matters most. The farm that plans from its records improves every season, and the improving farm compounds its gains.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Digital Records and Data Security',
                'summary' => 'Keep farm data safe and usable.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Digital records are only as good as their safety, and the grower who protects their data keeps the farm's memory intact. A lost log is a lost season, and a leaked record is a privacy problem, so data security is a farm skill now.\n\nStart with the platform's records: your profile, your documents, and your transaction history are stored on the platform and protected by your account. Protect the account with a strong, unique password, a locked phone, and a phone number only you control. The account is the gate to the farm's data.\n\nNext, keep your own digital records backed up. If you keep a farm log or a yield spreadsheet on a phone or a computer, copy it regularly to a second place: a cloud account, a memory card, or a printed copy. The backup is the record that survives the broken screen, the lost phone, or the failed harvest of the device itself.\n\nGuard personal data. Worker IDs, phone numbers, and bank details are sensitive; store them securely and share them only with those who need them for payment or verification. The grower who protects the workers' data keeps the crew's trust and the cooperative's standards.\n\nFinally, keep records usable: a fixed format, clear dates, and consistent names for blocks and products. The record that is safe but unreadable is a record that cannot be used. The grower who keeps records safe, backed up, and readable owns a farm memory that improves every season.",
