    148 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Understanding Transaction Receipts',
                'summary' => 'Read every receipt like a contract.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Every transaction on the platform produces a receipt, and the user who reads the receipt catches errors before they become disputes. A receipt is a contract in small print, and it takes a minute to read.\n\nStart with the reference. The transaction reference is the unique key to the payment, and every question about the payment starts with it. Quote the reference in any support request, and keep the receipt until the transaction is fully settled. The reference turns a vague complaint into a traceable case.\n\nNext, read the amount, the status, and the parties. The amount should match what you intended; the status should be completed, pending, or failed; and the parties should be you and the expected recipient or platform. A receipt that shows a different amount, a different recipient, or a failed status is a receipt that needs action now, not later.\n\nThen read the timing. The timestamp shows when the platform recorded the transaction, and the settlement timing shows when the funds move. Wallet payments settle instantly; card refunds take several business days. The user who knows the timing does not panic during the gap, and does not double-pay while a pending transaction completes.\n\nFinally, reconcile your receipts weekly against your own notes. The weekly reconciliation catches the duplicate charge, the missing refund, and the hold that never resolved, while they are still fresh and easy to trace. The receipt read and kept is the payment proven.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Payment Problems and How to Escalate',
                'summary' => 'The ladder from stuck payment to resolution.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Payment problems are rare when the flow is used correctly, but every user should know the escalation ladder, because a stuck payment needs the right step at the right time, not a panic.\n\nStart with the transactions list. Most 'missing money' reports are holds or pending entries that resolve on their own. Check the status first: pending means the transaction is in flight, held means the funds are reserved, and failed means no money moved. The list answers most questions without any contact.\n\nNext, give it the time the receipt says. Bank transfers matched manually can take up to one business day; card refunds take several. A payment that is inside its expected window is not yet a problem, and retrying too early creates duplicate pending holds that make the balance look smaller than it is.\n\nThen escalate to the support desk with the full evidence: the transaction reference, the amount, the timestamp, and a screenshot. Support can trace the payment in the system and escalate to the finance team when needed. A well-documented ticket is answered fastest, and the reference is the thread the finance team pulls.\n\nFinally, if a marketplace payment is stuck because of a dispute, use the platform's dispute process rather than the seller's phone. The platform holds the payment until the issue is resolved, and that hold is the leverage that resolves the case fairly. The user who escalates with evidence, in order, is the user whose payment problems end.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    152 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Negotiating Terms and Contracts',
                'summary' => 'Negotiate terms that both sides can keep.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "The best procurement agreements are the ones both sides can keep, and the buyer who negotiates clear terms avoids the disputes that follow vague promises. Negotiation on the platform is about terms, not just price.\n\nStart with the total cost, not the headline price. Freight, loading, packaging, and payment fees are real costs, and the buyer who negotiates the delivered price knows what the purchase actually costs. A low headline price with hidden costs is the most expensive kind of quote.\n\nNegotiate the terms that matter: the grade and specification, the quantity and tolerance, the delivery date and location, and the payment method. Write every agreed term into the platform order, because the platform order is the record both sides rely on if anything is disputed. An oral term is a term that evaporates.\n\nUse competition honestly. Send the request for quote to several shortlisted suppliers, compare like with like, and let the comparison inform the negotiation. The supplier who knows a comparable quote exists prices honestly, and the buyer who has a backup does not negotiate from need.\n\nFinally, keep the relationship in view. Terms that squeeze the supplier to the point of failure come back as shortfalls and bad quality. The buyer who negotiates terms the supplier can profitably keep gets reliable supply for years, and reliable supply beats a cheap price every season.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Managing Delivery and Receipt',
                'summary' => 'Inspect, confirm, and protect every delivery.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "The delivery is the moment the purchase becomes real, and the buyer who manages delivery and receipt properly protects their money and their operation. The platform's hold-and-release flow is the protection, and the inspection is how you use it.\n\nStart before the delivery: confirm the date, the quantity, and the grade with the supplier, and prepare the receiving: the space, the scales, the labour, and the time. A delivery that arrives to an unprepared farm is a delivery that gets damaged while everyone scrambles.\n\nAt delivery, inspect before you confirm. Check the grade against the order, count or weigh the quantity, and note the condition. Photograph the consignment at handover, especially for fresh produce, and note any shortfall or damage on the delivery record. The inspection is the evidence, and the evidence is the protection.\n\nConfirm only when the goods match the order. Confirmation releases the payment to the seller, and a confirmed payment for short or damaged goods is the hardest payment to recover. If the delivery is wrong, do not confirm: raise it immediately in the platform with photos and the order reference, and follow the dispute process.\n\nFinally, record the receipt in your own procurement log: the order, the delivered quantity and grade, the condition, and the supplier's handling. The buyer who manages receipt this way builds the supplier scorecard that makes every future order safer.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    203 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Preparing Your First Service Offerings',
                'summary' => 'Package and price your first services.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "A service is harder to list than a product, because the buyer cannot see it before they buy. The provider who packages and prices their first services clearly gets the inquiries and the bookings, and the one who does not gets the confusion.\n\nStart by defining the service precisely. What exactly do you do, to what, where, and to what standard? 'Spraying' means nothing until you state the crops covered, the method, the products used, and the safety standards followed. A precise definition is what lets the buyer decide, and it is what lets you price the work.\n\nNext, price the service from its cost: labour, travel, equipment, materials, and the margin that keeps the business alive. Check what comparable services list for in your region, and price to be competitive without pricing below cost. A service priced below cost is a service that ends with the first job.\n\nState the booking process and the lead time: how the buyer books, how far in advance, what the buyer should prepare, and how payment works through the platform. The listing that answers those four questions converts inquiries into bookings, and the platform payment protects both sides.\n\nFinally, deliver the first service to the written standard, and ask for feedback. The first service done to standard, with evidence and a follow-up, is the one that becomes your review, and your first review is the beginning of your store's reputation.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Handling Your First Customer Order',
                'summary' => 'Fulfil the first order to a standard.',
                'duration' => 30,
                'content' => "Your first order is the one that sets the store's reputation, and the provider who fulfils it to a standard turns a first buyer into a regular. The standard is a checklist, and the checklist is the difference between a smooth order and a stressful one.\n\nStart by confirming the order in writing: the product or service, the quantity, the grade, the price, and the delivery terms. The platform order record is the confirmation, and the order that is confirmed in writing is the order that cannot be misremembered. Read it back to yourself before you commit.\n\nNext, prepare against real stock. Confirm you have the quantity and the grade before you promise, and if stock is short, tell the buyer immediately with the options: partial delivery, a new date, or a refund. The provider who communicates a problem early is the provider the buyer forgives; the one who goes silent is the one the buyer reports.\n\nFulfil to the standard: the right product, packed or performed to the standard, dispatched with a photo, and delivered on the agreed date. Share the tracking or the pickup details promptly, and keep the evidence of dispatch. The evidence is the record that settles any dispute in seconds.\n\nFinally, close the order properly: confirm delivery with the buyer, request feedback, and file the record. The first order closed cleanly is the template for the second, and the provider who repeats the standard grows a store that sells itself.",
